feat: horizontal brush stroke behind "biggest"; new brand yellow

The word goes back to being live type at the headline's own size — the
previous lockup was an image of the word, so it rendered at a different
size to its neighbours. The brush now sits behind it as a background
instead, and the word measures 192px at 1440 and 68px at 425, matching
the lines around it exactly.

The stroke is rotated in the asset rather than in CSS. The source art
runs diagonally, and levelling it with a transform would rotate the
box along with the paint, tilting the word. The angle was measured off
the artwork rather than guessed — the covariance of its opaque pixels
puts the principal axis at -55.5 degrees, so the asset is rotated
55.5 degrees once at build time, trimmed to the paint, and the box
stays square to the line. A first attempt at -52 degrees left it
portrait, which is what prompted measuring it properly.

Background-size is 100% 100% rather than contain: contain preserves the
art's ratio, and against a short wide word that collapsed the stroke to
the line height and read as a smudge behind two letters. The white
ground is flood-filled from the edges, so the dry-brush gaps inside the
stroke stay transparent instead of filling in.

The brand yellow moves from #f0a202 to #fbc903 across the site. The
token drives almost everything, so this is one value in globals.css
plus four hardcoded uses in the podcast cover themes and the meter.
Contrast improves: ink on honey goes from 9.31:1 to 12.69:1, and the
recorded figures in the token comment are updated to match. Yellow
still never carries text — at 1.56:1 on white it is fills, marks and
the logo only.
